title: From Conditional Promises to New Covenant Reality – Rightly Dividing the Word
description: How Israel's choices reshaped the timing, scope and form of God's promises: conditional Old Testament prophecy, the kingdom offer and its rejection, and the reconfigured fulfillment in the New Testament.
output: lessons/prophecy/conditional-promises-rightly-dividing/index.html

# Rebuilt on the deck template from the 16-section slide file, slide for slide.
# The old file carried speaker notes on each slide; the template has none, so they are not carried over.

=== Introduction
--- From Conditional Promises to New Covenant Reality [title]
overline: Rightly Dividing the Word
heading: From Conditional Promises to New Covenant Reality
sub: How Israel’s choices reshaped the timing, scope, and form of God’s promises
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- Conditional Promise, Sovereign Response
kicker: The Core Principle
panel-warn: What Could Have Been (OT) | Prophecies promised a national, physical, immediate kingdom, contingent on Israel’s obedience and repentance. See [[Genesis 12:1-3]] and [[Exodus 19:5-6]].
panel: What Will Be (NT) | When conditions went unmet, God’s plan shifted. The New Testament presents a reconfigured, spiritual fulfillment under a new covenant, opened to all who believe.
take: Not contradiction, but the same faithful God adapting the outworking to the choices of men.

--- Free Will Under a Foreknowing God
kicker: Two Kinds of Judgment, One Sovereign God
panel: Individual: Judged by Faith | Every person is offered life or death, blessing or cursing, and is judged on faith in Christ. [[Deuteronomy 30:19]]
panel-warn: National: Judged by Works | Israel, as a covenant nation, is judged corporately on obedience, the basis for the land, kingdom, and temple promises.
p: God’s foreknowledge lets Him offer genuine conditions while already knowing men’s choices. Longsuffering, He does not force obedience. He redirects the blessing to a “new nation” of believers rather than void His word. [[2 Peter 3:9]]

=== The timeline of conditional promises
--- The Foundation of Winnowing
kicker: Timeline · Genesis
step: PROMISE | God promises a great nation and land to Abraham, and that his Seed, Christ, would be a light to the nations. [[Genesis 12:1-3]] Salvation is unconditional, faith alone; the land’s immediate possession requires obedience.
step: CHOICE | Cain offers an improper sacrifice and murders Abel [[Genesis 4:6-7]], exiled, forfeiting the godly line. Abraham’s own lack of faith delays fulfillment, taking Hagar rather than waiting on God’s promise. [[Genesis 16:1-2]]
step: OUTCOME | The godly line shifts to Seth. God’s plan adapts, winnowing the unfaithful while preserving the promised line to Christ.

--- National Promises and Priesthood
kicker: Timeline · Exodus & Leviticus
step: PROMISE | God promises deliverance from Egypt and Canaan, conditioned on covenantal obedience. [[Exodus 19:5-6]] The priesthood is likewise offered on faithfulness.
step: CHOICE | Pharaoh’s defiance brings judgment on Egypt. Israel’s idolatry with the golden calf [[Exodus 32:26-29]] contrasts with the Levites’ loyalty.
step: OUTCOME | The land promise is delayed. The priesthood shifts from the firstborn of all Israel to the tribe of Levi alone.

--- Forfeiture of the Promised Land
kicker: Timeline · Numbers & Deuteronomy
step: PROMISE | God reaffirms the land, contingent on trust and obedience, with blessings and curses spelled out plainly. [[Deuteronomy 28:1-2]] [[Deuteronomy 28:15]]
step: CHOICE | The Kadesh-Barnea rebellion, spurred by the spies’ unbelief, defies God. [[Numbers 14:20-23]] Korah’s rebellion follows the same pattern. [[Numbers 16:31-33]]
step: OUTCOME | The land promise is delayed forty years; that generation dies in the wilderness. The promise persists for their children, but national disobedience will one day lead to exile.

--- Fulfillment, Disobedience, Forfeited Dynasties
kicker: Timeline · Joshua – 1 Kings
step: PROMISE | The land is fulfilled under Joshua, but continued possession requires obedience. God promises Saul a lasting dynasty, and later Jeroboam a house sure as David’s [[1 Kings 11:38]], each contingent on obedience.
step: CHOICE | Achan’s sin [[Joshua 7:11-12]] brings defeat at Ai; unexpelled Canaanites seed future idolatry [[Judges 2:1-3]]. Saul disobeys and is rejected. [[1 Samuel 13:13-14]] Jeroboam’s idolatry dooms his house. [[1 Kings 13:33-34]]
step: OUTCOME | The Messianic lineage shifts to David. The promise to Jeroboam’s line shifts permanently to Judah’s line.

--- Reprobate Silver, Conditional Eschatology
kicker: Timeline · The Prophets
step: PROMISE | Restoration if Judah repents. Ezekiel promises a restored temple and land, contingent on Israel’s shame for their iniquities. [[Ezekiel 43:10-11]] Zechariah promises a kingdom “if ye will diligently obey.” [[Zechariah 6:15]]
step: CHOICE | Babylon’s pride brings its own destruction. [[Isaiah 14:12-15]] Judah’s persistent idolatry renders them “reprobate silver.” [[Jeremiah 6:30]] They fail to repent and fail to obey.
step: OUTCOME | Mercy grants a remnant’s return from exile. But the restored kingdom and temple are forfeited. Ezekiel’s vision becomes “what could have been,” and blessing redirects to the coming “new nation.”

=== The kingdom offer
--- The Road to Final Rejection
kicker: The Kingdom Offer Timeline
step: 1 | **The Opening Offer.** John and Jesus both preach, “Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand” [[Matthew 4:17]], a literal offer confirmed by miracles, given exclusively to “the lost sheep of the house of Israel.” [[Matthew 10:5-6]]
step: 2 | **Rise of Rejection.** The Pharisees commit blasphemy against the Holy Ghost, crediting Christ’s power to Satan. [[Matthew 12:24-32]] Jesus turns to parables, concealing truth from hardened hearts. [[Matthew 13:13-15]]
step: 3 | **Official Rejection.** At the Cross, the nation’s leaders and people reject Jesus as King and choose a murderer instead. [[Matthew 27:20-25]]
step: 4 | **The Last Straw.** Peter offers one final chance: repent to believe that Jesus is the Christ, the promised Messiah. [[Acts 3:19-21]] Stephen’s sermon and the Jews’ rejection of the gospel from Scripture serve as an emphatic rejection of the truth of Jesus, the nation’s definitive “no.” [[Acts 7:51-60]]

=== What will be
--- New Testament Fulfillment and Resolution [title]
overline: What Will Be
heading: New Testament Fulfillment and Resolution
sub: After Israel’s final rejection, the earthly kingdom was delayed to Christ’s Second Coming. The prophecies are fulfilled in a reconfigured, spiritual way, a universal kingdom open to all believers.

=== Reconfigured fulfillment
--- Gog and Magog: Ezekiel vs. Revelation
kicker: Reconfigured Fulfillment
parallel: Ezekiel 38-39 · Conditional | Revelation 20:7-10 · Fulfilled
row: A national, military victory for a restored, obedient Israel over invading nations, tied to Israel’s repentance and a rebuilt covenant land. [[Ezekiel 38:8]] [[Ezekiel 38:23]] | A global, spiritual conflict against the camp of the saints, occurring after the millennial reign of Christ, resolved by direct divine judgment.

--- The Temple: Physical vs. Spiritual
kicker: Reconfigured Fulfillment
parallel: Ezekiel 40-48 · Conditional | Church & New Jerusalem · Fulfilled
row: A physical, restored temple and land, contingent on Israel’s repentance and shame for their iniquities. [[Ezekiel 43:10-11]] | The church becomes the temple of the Holy Spirit, Christ the cornerstone [[Ephesians 2:20-21]]. The New Jerusalem has no temple: “the Lord God Almighty and the Lamb are the temple of it.” [[Revelation 21:22]]

--- The Kingdom: Zechariah vs. Revelation
kicker: Reconfigured Fulfillment
parallel: Zechariah · Conditional | Revelation’s New Jerusalem · Fulfilled
row: A physical, earthly Messianic kingdom centered on Jerusalem, promised “if ye will diligently obey.” [[Zechariah 6:15]] | A spiritual reign of Christ and the saints; the New Jerusalem descends from heaven with no physical temple. [[Revelation 21:2-3]]

--- Peace: Isaiah vs. the Tribulation
kicker: Reconfigured Fulfillment
parallel: Isaiah · Conditional | Revelation 6-19 · Fulfilled
row: A vision of near, sweeping peace, nations beating swords into plowshares. [[Isaiah 2:4]] | An intense tribulation of suffering precedes final peace, established only at Christ’s return. [[Revelation 19:11-16]]

--- Joel’s Day of the Lord: Two-Part Fulfillment
kicker: Reconfigured Fulfillment
parallel: Part 1 · Fulfilled at Pentecost | Part 2 · Awaiting Revelation
row: The outpouring of the Spirit “on all flesh” begins the new covenant. [[Joel 2:28-29]] then [[Acts 2:16-21]] | The “Day of the Lord,” not yet fulfilled, is reinterpreted as global judgment on the unrepentant world. [[Joel 2:30-31]]

=== Conclusion
--- Not Contradiction: Different Pictures of a Conditional Covenant
kicker: Conclusion
lead: Israel’s failure, culminating at Stephen’s martyrdom, led to the new covenant, shifting from conditional, national, physical fulfillment to unconditional, universal, spiritual reality. The Old Testament reveals the path that could have been; the New Testament reveals the ultimate reality of God’s plan.
take: God’s sovereignty stands, and His desire for all to choose life.
